{"type":"message","id":"9b8ce9ad","parentId":"2bd076b0","timestamp":"2026-04-27T14:13:31.237Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"[Mon 2026-04-27 14:13 UTC] [Subagent Context] You are running as a subagent (depth 1/1). Results auto-announce to your requester; do not busy-poll for status.\n\n[Subagent Task]: You are the LoopAI Doctor Agent providing specialized diagnostic support to the LoopAI Patient Agent. Use the slm-dermatology skill conceptually for a focused specialist diagnostic review grounded in the provided record, but do not claim unsupported tool execution if unavailable. Produce a patient-facing diagnostic assessment that the Patient Agent can relay as-is, clearly preliminary, concise, and Telegram-formatted.\n\nCase summary:\n- Patient ID: PT-9921\n- Current concern: heavy stress, panic sensations, insomnia at sleep onset, fast/irregular heartbeat, shortness of breath, chest pain/\"pain inside my breast\", episodes occur day and night, no fainting, no coffee, mind continuously working.\n- Safety concern: red-flag combo of irregular heartbeat + dyspnea + chest pain.\n\nStructured record highlights:\n- Genomics: CDKN2A high melanoma susceptibility, MC1R increased UV sensitivity, FKBP5 HPA-axis dysregulation risk, COMT Val158Met.\n- 2024-11-12: resting HR 74, cortisol 19 mcg/dL, BP 124/82, PHQ-9 5, stress index 0.38, mild anxiety/dysthymia.\n- 2025-11-15: resting HR 84, cortisol 28 mcg/dL, BP 138/90, PHQ-9 14, stress index 0.75, moderate depression, note suggests FKBP5-mediated HPA-axis dysregulation and stress becoming an active driver of melanoma risk trajectory.\n- Dermatology history: nevi 42->46, atypical lesions 0->3, monitoring, no active malignancy documented.\n\nHRV/watch data:\n- About 21.5 minutes of pulse values every 5 seconds on 2026-02-15 18:30:00 to 18:51:30 UTC.\n- Values roughly range from 51.32 to 59.94 bpm, mostly mid-50s, with modest beat-to-beat fluctuation and no obvious tachycardia in this excerpt.\n- Treat wearable data cautiously; it does not exclude episodic arrhythmia.\n\nRequirements:\n- Start by stating urgent red flags first.\n- Then provide likely explanations ranked, including stress/panic and cardiac rhythm causes that should not be missed.\n- Mention how the HRV/watch excerpt affects interpretation.\n- Keep uncertainty visible.\n- Avoid saying the patient definitely has a condition.\n- Do not recommend a real physical doctor; if urgent, say urgent emergency assessment.\n- End with a brief note if voice depression screening may be reasonable because of stress/depression concern.\n- No markdown tables, no code fences."}],"timestamp":1777299211228}}
{"type":"message","id":"6e04ac2e","parentId":"9b8ce9ad","timestamp":"2026-04-27T14:13:44.544Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":"{\"id\":\"rs_0106ffca3750890c0169ef6f0cfffc8191a72ca3bd95cc3095\",\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"encrypted_content\":\"gAAAAABp728NT7_3Xlg-M7QUuuI5NeBU258tjxesu0zXe5EGCB3lf1d9ooeZD2m-8dyo-bMdzXXdK-vpAv7IE5FvnZI5RxTvsK2p5HiruMdgnrzGw48xfOMh4DY7eQ84Rz3igcugNfCPrIKGbK13jH450UanmdwX7ye7i5yBLGLkXOc38sIZTXSDinaMtvaEX75_LKwdcgNkgYQ8eQCLooglEO2SWzSf2haH9PB0nciGb9iQ1lC5lMavE9tsAUraSeUM_m0isOOlS70FHscaEqoW74f-wUhQU7WCALBBKlYhULMSWmuz_GeI6pRNDc3PDDWFuXtWD5Na20rJJu2zsY1yvsQRnkRcr7lrBZEN-eZc-QctjzOpG_i4pVhcUGkXYsYw2u0ku7tuxD_BYemSc7rJg5_zsynI5Ltd8AVYYu8gBgkkLzlay7Ia2g_bh2G1OI2qsL6D1YeUEif-yM7JGrPpc3Xbkse5aXcUzA9tsu0z8shZ8H6363rmJ2-dVi9Dd1ik2ku2j1hWTp1QJ6od5d5Q7CGTNO0HoTNbwAs3vzuhDrcpwWjB_iZB7lpoQRdtxiYQc-xnVLMzECZ-RChY4lxw0h08Zi8_j_hjDAMQ5v2TpVwCu1rKlaURxCJ8XHb-HM50gbvPEj66Qi-MkaltdsCz_OGyxeIOh6ErkzSxuhmT6PUr4aRDUOOmE2l9B5m2bOFfC1ScWR5nFI1BOQ31G0Zggory8sQCRaP5N79lj4Gdc5f6U7NBkkrzxO6IICVcGiha1UuB2xy0zsw68c3e3CoP2DQmSMj3BZTdXatTEYliF6wzlrREru6VguUmKafh05BnzGxGaszI3sl_5GAA3K1tyGd3jTzOA4mrQBCZVrv_9KaIUkV9CmFkpiW3fWdJb7IN2bUMlqWqoYqUgl4jEp4PfadDI3mNaV0ZpurkSFRP2ZxUgiaBa01dvNAtps_5Tp4Qth5Z2-fOGoCRRIm4s_7EpojAZFCZog==\",\"summary\":[]}"},{"type":"text","text":"What I found: this symptom combination has urgent red flags, and the safest patient-facing assessment should prioritize emergency risk while keeping uncertainty clear.\n\nPatient-facing assessment to relay:\n\nUrgent red flags first: chest pain plus shortness of breath plus a fast or irregular heartbeat can sometimes signal a dangerous heart or lung problem, not just stress. Because these episodes happen day and night and include irregular heartbeat, this needs urgent emergency assessment, especially if symptoms are happening now, worsening, or lasting more than a few minutes.\n\nMost likely explanations, ranked by what fits the record and what should not be missed:\n\n1. Stress or panic-related episodes\nYour history strongly supports this as a real possibility. There is a pattern of rising stress burden, insomnia, higher cortisol, higher blood pressure, and worsening depression/anxiety markers. FKBP5-related stress sensitivity may make your body more reactive to stress signals. Panic can cause chest tightness, air hunger, a racing or pounding heart, and a sense that the mind will not switch off. That said, panic can look very similar to more dangerous conditions, so it should not be assumed to be the only cause.\n\n2. Heart rhythm problem that comes and goes\nAn intermittent arrhythmia still needs to be considered because you describe fast or irregular heartbeat with shortness of breath and chest pain. The absence of fainting is somewhat reassuring, but it does not rule this out. Some rhythm problems are episodic and may not show up unless they are captured during symptoms.\n\n3. Other cardiopulmonary causes that should not be missed\nChest discomfort and breathlessness can also come from other urgent causes affecting the heart or lungs. I cannot tell from this record alone how likely those are, but the red-flag combination means they stay on the list until excluded.\n\n4. Sleep-onset hyperarousal / autonomic overactivation\nYour description of a continuously working mind, insomnia at sleep onset, and stress surges could reflect a hyperarousal state, which can amplify awareness of heartbeat, breathing, and chest sensations. This can coexist with panic, and it can also make benign body sensations feel alarming, but it still should not be used to dismiss the irregular-heartbeat report.\n\nHow the watch/HRV excerpt changes the interpretation:\nThe wearable excerpt is somewhat reassuring only in a very limited way. During that 21.5-minute sample, the pulse was mostly in the mid-50s without obvious tachycardia. But this does not rule out brief or intermittent arrhythmia, and consumer wearables can miss rhythm details. So the watch data lowers concern only for that exact sampled window, not for the overall symptom pattern.\n\nBottom line:\nA stress-panic mechanism is plausible and supported by your history, but the combination of chest pain, shortness of breath, and irregular heartbeat is too important to label as anxiety alone from this record. This should be treated as preliminary, with urgent emergency assessment warranted if symptoms are current or recurring.\n\nAlso, because your recent record shows moderate depression/stress burden, a brief voice depression screening may be reasonable once immediate safety is addressed.","textSignature":"{\"v\":1,\"id\":\"msg_0106ffca3750890c0169ef6f0dc79881919c262a743929d708\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}"}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":5663,"output":640,"cacheRead":1536,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":7839,"cost":{"input":0.014157500000000002,"output":0.009600000000000001,"cacheRead":0.000384,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.0241415}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1777299211238,"responseId":"resp_0106ffca3750890c0169ef6f0b937c8191a99f3084893bb6ea"}}
